_0)$ represent?","options":["The values of all the input variables of the function.","A finite subcover of the domain.","The boundary of the image.","The transpose of the gradient of one component function $f_i$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0161fa7025f70676b7954fd3","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_jacobian_matrix_0e73a2bb_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{f}\\colon\\mathbb{R}^3\\to\\mathbb{R}^2$. What is the size of $\\boldsymbol{\\mathrm{J}}\\boldsymbol{f}(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$?","options":["$3\\times 2$","$3\\times 3$","$2\\times 3$","$2\\times 2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"15a089c3557ea4c375b80c29","time_limit":20,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_jacobian_matrix_0e73a2bb_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the condition that $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is an interior point natural for defining the Jacobian?","options":["Partial derivatives vary one coordinate in both directions near $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$.","The Jacobian is defined only for boundary points.","Interior points make all partial derivatives automatically equal to zero.","The Jacobian needs no nearby values of the function."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5bd377aeab00f9f87126ea76","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}